+## 28 October review follow-up: API and verification
+Constructor now appends `TimeSpan? operationTimeout = null` after maxResponseBytes. Default 60 seconds, valid `(0, 10 minutes]`; invalid values throw ArgumentOutOfRangeException. Added CirclebackErrorKind.TimedOut. Other public signatures and DTO shapes are unchanged.
+
+Each public operation owns one linked deadline spanning asynchronous token acquisition, send, stream acquisition/read, JSON parsing and nested validation. WaitAsync bounds non-cooperative asynchronous accessors/transports/reads. Final cancellation is checked after validation before return. Caller cancellation returns a fresh OperationCanceledException with caller token; internal expiry returns sanitized TimedOut. Other accessor failures become sanitized Unavailable without inner exceptions. The default HttpClient timeout is infinite because the operation deadline is authoritative. Late successful send responses are disposed.
+
+ID validation is shared for requested and returned IDs. Present attendee/assignee records require positive ProfileId and nonblank Name; present tags require positive Id/nonblank Name; actions require positive Id/nonblank Title. Optional collections and nullable fields remain optional; no enum/status vocabulary is inferred. This intentionally strict projected contract can reject provider records missing required identity data. Unknown fields remain ignored and provider text/URLs remain inert untrusted data.
+
+Link parsing recognizes exact space-separated relation tokens (registered relation matching case-insensitively), quoted strings/escaping, optional HTTP whitespace, multiple parameters and comma-separated link values. Absolute, path-relative and query-relative next targets resolve against the request, then pass fixed HTTPS origin/path validation. Multiple next targets and malformed Link syntax are refused. Only next targets are navigable; other relations are ignored. No automatic pagination/retry.
+
+Limits: synchronous accessor/handler execution, CPU-bound bounded JSON/validation and synchronous Dispose cannot be forcibly preempted; deadline checks detect expiry before success. Non-cooperative asynchronous work may outlive the returned timeout; accessors/custom handlers are trusted infrastructure and must honor cancellation for prompt underlying cleanup. There is no attempt to terminate arbitrary injected code. Default transport disables redirects/cookies; arbitrary custom handlers must not independently redirect/forward credentials.
